{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
  "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: D. KENT SAFRIET, ESQ., Tallahassee, Fla.; on behalf of the Petitioner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: I thought your basic position in the litigation in Florida was that the Florida legislation violated the takings protection, and so it's kind of strange to switch your target from the legislature, which enacted this measure, and then say, because the judiciary upheld it, the judiciary somehow is complicit in this violation by the legislature implemented by the administrative offices."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Well, they're -- they're eliminated -- at least the right of contact with the water can be eliminated by an avulsion, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: If somebody wanted to put up a hot dog stand on this new land, would you have the right to tell them they can't?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SAFRIET: Yes, the legislature can change this definition of property. They can define property in the State of Florida as to whatever they want it to be, but if they do so and take property, they must pay for it under the just compensation clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Let me ask you this question on Florida valuation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Did anybody -- I'm not sure it's a bad deal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. SAFRIET",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SAFRIET: I think that's the fundamental\nmisunderstanding in this case. The beach was not\neroding. It was not lapping under these houses. There\nwas 200 feet of dry beach, and the beach is accreting,\nmeaning it grows gradually, day by day or week by week."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MAKAR",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MAKAR: Well, it would be an avulsion. It would still be the State adding sand to its side of the line."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Would that person still be\nconsidered a littoral owner?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Makar",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Absolutely, absolutely. And\nthat's a major misnomer in this case, is that the upland\nowner here, even after the beach restoration project,\nhas riparian littoral property. That's what the Florida\nSupreme Court has held, that's what the Florida\nlegislation says."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, suppose that --\nsuppose that a city decided that it wanted to attract\nmore students who are going to the beach in Florida for\nspring break, and so therefore it decided it was going\nto create a huge beach in front of -- of privately owned\nhomes. Under the decision of the Florida Supreme Court,\nI don't see anything that would stop the city from doing\nthat."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Would you have answered Justice Scalia's question that there's a common -- a generally understood common law right, littoral right, to contact with the water? Would you say there is not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: It could be -- it could be adding an amusement park, though, under your theory, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They have a right, not only under Florida common law to own to the mean high-water line; under their deeds, that's what they purchased."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SAFRIET: Temporarily, Your Honor, because under the doctrine of reclamation, they can reclaim the boundary line that they lost, just as if in a case where the hurricane washes sand away, the landowner, under common law, the doctrine of reclamation, can bring in sand where the water is to reconnect to that mean high-water line that would be underwater following a hurricane."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1151",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c5b75ef7ee888e72ddce64dfd4283758034c0194a481452b905e581fd921c08",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c5b75ef7ee8-0003-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The case is submitted."
    },
    {
